edit - change a text and write it to a file

  edit <file>            open it, or start it if it is not there
  edit                   a blank sheet
  log | edit             whatever is piped in, in a sheet of its own

  arrows, Home, End      move; Ctrl+Home and Ctrl+End for the whole file
  Page Up, Page Down     a screen at a time
  Enter, Backspace, Del  the usual
  Ctrl+S                 save, asking for a name if there is none yet
  Ctrl+O                 open another file
  Ctrl+W                 fold long lines instead of scrolling sideways
  Ctrl+N                 number the lines
  Ctrl+Q, Esc            leave

Nothing is written until asked, so a file opened and looked at is a file
left alone. The bar says which file, where the cursor is, and marks the
name with a star while there is unsaved work; leaving with work unsaved
asks once before it does.

A text with no file behind it reads as `(unnamed)` and is otherwise an
ordinary document: it is what a blank sheet and a pipe both give, and
saving one asks where it should go. Opening another file over unsaved
work is refused rather than silently losing it.

A file longer than the editor holds is opened read-only in effect: it
says so, and saving is refused rather than writing back the head of a
file and losing its tail.

Lines wider than the console scroll sideways rather than wrapping, so a
row on screen is always a line in the file. `Ctrl+W` folds them instead,
and then a line may take several rows; the number column marks only the
row a line starts on, so what is numbered is still what is in the file.

A line running off the edge ends in a dim arrow, and one scrolled past
on the left begins with one, so a cut line and a line that happens to
end there are never the same picture.

Tabs are typed as spaces. The console draws a tab as one cell, and a
cursor counting it as several would sit somewhere the text is not.

Accented characters arrive as whatever the current layout makes of the
key, the same as everywhere else; `man vibeee` describes switching it.
